{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "curvature_change",
      "parent_relation": "The parent maps reflected power only through coherent phase accumulation phi_layer and the three real lossless impedances, so for fixed Z_1 and Z_2 the dependence on Z_layer is the symmetric lossless Fabry-Perot curve with exact interchange symmetry and full coherent extrema.",
      "child_relation": "Relax the perfectly lossless interlayer assumption so the interlayer propagation phase becomes effectively complex; the target remains reflected power, but its dependence on phi_layer and Z_layer is damped by internal attenuation, reducing fringe visibility and changing curvature near constructive and destructive interference conditions.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "Z_1",
        "Z_2",
        "Z_layer",
        "phi_layer"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "At fixed Z_1, Z_2, and mismatch ratios, sweeping phi_layer no longer gives the parent’s full-contrast oscillation; minima are lifted and maxima flattened, with the strongest deviation when both interface reflections are appreciable and round-trip phase is near odd or even multiples of pi."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "r12",
        "expression": "(Z_layer - Z_1)/(Z_layer + Z_1)",
        "role": "front-face amplitude reflection coefficient"
      },
      {
        "name": "r23",
        "expression": "(Z_2 - Z_layer)/(Z_2 + Z_layer)",
        "role": "back-face amplitude reflection coefficient"
      },
      {
        "name": "eta_fp",
        "expression": "((Z_layer - Z_1)/(Z_layer + Z_1))*((Z_2 - Z_layer)/(Z_2 + Z_layer))",
        "role": "round-trip interface-coupling strength controlling interference visibility"
      },
      {
        "name": "phase_scan",
        "expression": "cos(phi_layer)",
        "role": "coherent phase coordinate already sampled by the parent mapping"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when the interlayer is thick enough or lossy enough that one round trip incurs non-negligible attenuation, while both interfaces still have appreciable mismatch so internal multiple reflections matter; negligible in the nearly transparent or vanishing-thickness limit.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, moderate mismatch and phase sweeps would already show reduced contrast relative to the ideal lossless formula; in holdout, larger effective optical thickness extends this to stronger suppression of oscillatory curvature without changing the basic three-medium geometry.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent law in the zero-loss limit of the interlayer, where round-trip attenuation tends to unity and the propagation factor becomes purely phase.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model treats the coherent interlayer as exactly lossless, so multiple-reflection interference has undamped visibility; it omits physically admissible attenuation inside the layer that modifies the scored reflection mapping itself, not just unobserved field amplitudes.",
    "baseline_assumption": "The parent assumes the interlayer medium is lossless, giving a purely real wave impedance and a purely phase-valued round-trip propagation factor.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Weak absorption or finite conductivity in the interlayer causes the internal round-trip factor to decay in magnitude as well as rotate in phase, damping Fabry-Perot interference in the reflected power.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "Z_1",
      "Z_2",
      "Z_layer",
      "phi_layer"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax only the lossless-interlayer assumption already used by the parent. The parent response relationship is a fully coherent three-medium reflection law in which, for fixed Z_1 and Z_2, varying phi_layer produces ideal undamped interference governed by the product r12 r23. The child changes the target mapping itself: at the same supplied Z_1, Z_2, Z_layer, and phi_layer coordinates, the reflected-power curve versus phi_layer acquires reduced fringe contrast and altered curvature because each internal round trip is attenuated before interfering with the prompt reflection. This is observable by varying phi_layer while holding endpoint impedances fixed and using a mismatch regime with |r12 r23| not small; the parent with any single parameter set cannot reproduce a systematic lifting of destructive-interference minima together with flattening of maxima across those conditions if it is constrained to remain lossless. The effect is scientifically admissible for conductive or weakly absorbing films and disappears continuously in the zero-loss limit. Limitation: under the present freedom contract no extra sampled variable can be introduced to quantify loss strength, so the eventual implementation would need a fixed refinement rather than a newly varied environment; nevertheless the omitted mechanism is genuine and changes the scored mapping when active."
  },
